      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Let $x$ be the integer we are looking for. We want to find the only integer whose square is less than its double, so we can set up the following inequality:\n$x^2 < 2x$\n\nTo solve this inequality, we can subtract $x^2$ from both sides:\n$0 < 2x - x^2$\n\nWe can factor out an $x$ from the right side:\n$0 < x(2 - x)$\n\nSince we are looking for an integer solution, we can set up a table to test different integer values for $x$:\n$x$ | $2 - x$ | $x(2 - x)$\n-----|----------|----------------\n-2   | 4       | 0\n-1   | 3       | -2\n0    | 2       | 0\n1    | 1       | -1\n2    | 0       | 0\n3    | -1      | -3\n4    | -2      | -8\n...\n\nWe can see that the only integer solution is $x = 1$, since for all other values of $x$, $x(2 - x)$ is negative or zero.\n\nTherefore, the only integer whose square is less than its double is $\\boxed{1}$.\nThe answer is: 1",
